Z||000006||Rodrv6||03-14-2001||01:55 PM||||You might want to look around for a vacuum leak.  Check all the hoses and make sure they are on tight and not split.  Also, check the EGR valve.  The one on my Duke was loose (bolts were backed out a little) and it caused a higher than normal idle.  You might try plugging the idle air passage on the throttle body and check the idle--should be down around 700 rpm or so.  If it's still high--definate vacuum leak.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000007||KRMFiero||03-14-2001||02:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rodrv6:<BR><B>You might try plugging the idle air passage on the throttle body and check the idle--should be down around 700 rpm or so.  If it's still high--definate vacuum leak.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sounds like a good way to cheak (this is going to make me sound dumb)  where is the idle air passage?||||reg||1
Z||000008||KRMFiero||03-14-2001||09:45 PM||||What size vacume lines does to 2.5 use?||||reg||1
Z||000009||voyagerspe||03-14-2001||10:11 PM||||Sound like vacumme to me.  Check the lines under the throttle body to see if there disconected or cracked.  Also a little trick to find vacumme leaks is take a propane torch<BR>with it UNLIT !!repeat do not light the torch!! with only the propane on and the car running trace the vac. lines with the unlit torch.  When the engine rpms change you found your leak.  This works by the crack in the vac. line sucking in the propane and changing the idle of the motor.  PLEASE be careful with the torch, I dont want anyone melting there cars.||||reg||1
Z||000010||MontanaFiero||03-14-2001||10:16 PM||||did you clean out the seat where the IAC goes in. Dirt can keep it  from closing all the way. Also, they made two of those and they were not interchangeable. consult the emissions section of your Chilton guide to determine which one you --- well, umm-- needed.  And follow the instructions on installation because you can ruin it easily too. Good luck, because those are expensive bastards!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg">   <BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- <I>project</I> &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
